  "summary": "Four mouse studies link neonatal, maternal, genetic, or environmental perturbations to cell-type-specific changes in cortex, hippocampus, and prefrontal circuits.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is cell-type-resolved readout of mouse brain after a defined perturbation. Neonatal SARS-CoV-2 spike protein exposure was associated with region-specific changes in SOX2 cells and TBR1 neurons at P10, without broad cerebrovascular or BBB alterations. Constitutive 5-HT2A deletion in BALB/c mice was linked to anxiety-like behavior, reduced hippocampal parvalbumin, and lower energy-metabolism protein levels. An enriched environment raised prefrontal perineuronal nets through NRG1/ErbB4 signaling and aggrecan, with improved cognition in adult mice. In the one published study, maternal TPOAb exposure caused placental inflammation, fetal BBB disruption, hippocampal microglial activation, and altered BDNF/synaptic signaling. The new element is the focus on specific cell populations, including progenitors, interneurons, and microglia, though most evidence is preprint-stage and associative.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
